<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heshen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1750">1750</a>–<a href="/years/1799">1799</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Hešen, Shanbao, Zhizhai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heshen was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, of the Niohuru clan and the Plain Red Banner, who rose from imperial bodyguard to become the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a>'s favourite, holding several top posts at once. His son married the emperor's favourite daughter in 1790, and by Qianlong's abdication in 1796 he had become practically a regent. He extorted gifts, filled posts with his own men and stole public and military funds, and has been described as the most corrupt official in Chinese history. After Qianlong died in 1799 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaqing-emperor">Jiaqing Emperor</a> charged him with 20 crimes, confiscated a fortune estimated at 60 to 80 million taels and ordered him to hang himself.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heshen.jpg" alt="Heshen" width="330" height="613" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hesen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Heshen was born on 1 July 1750 as Shanbao, and his name was later changed to Heshen. His courtesy name was Zhizhai. He was the son of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> military officer of the Plain Red Banner and studied at the Palace of Universal Peace, a school for the children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">Bannermen</a>. He lost his mother young, and it was said that he and his younger brother had a hard life under their stepmother. He was an excellent student who knew Middle Mandarin, Manchu, Mongolian and Tibetan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Heshen began work as an imperial bodyguard in 1772 and in 1775 was transferred to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a>, where he met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a>. Within a year he was junior vice-president of the Board of Revenue, then a Grand Councillor, and then Minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-household-department">Imperial Household Department</a>. He held several top posts at once, which gave him wide influence over the bureaucracy. In 1790 his son married the emperor's tenth and favourite daughter, Princess Hexiao. His power grew as the emperor aged, and after Qianlong's abdication in 1796, and especially after the death of his opponent Agui in 1797, he was practically a regent.</p><p>The records describe him as openly corrupt. He influenced practically every appointment in the country, filled key military and civil posts with his own men, demanded gifts from officials who owed him their places, and took public and military funds and tax revenue. Taxes were raised repeatedly, and officials who pocketed money meant for canals and dams are said to have contributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> flooding 17 times between 1797 and 1819.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Qianlong died on 7 February 1799. His successor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaqing-emperor">Jiaqing Emperor</a>, had Heshen arrested on 12 February, and an edict of 15 February declared him guilty of 20 crimes, including abuse of power, disrespect to imperial prerogatives and hoarding wealth. He was condemned to slow slicing, but out of respect for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> and for Princess Hexiao the emperor ordered him to hang himself, which he did on 22 February. His confiscated movable property was estimated at 60 to 80 million taels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, roughly a year of Qing revenue, with higher estimates disputed by historians.</p><p>Historians such as Daniel McMahon argue that the image of Heshen as a classic villain was contrived by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaqing-emperor">Jiaqing Emperor</a>. Before 1799 official records portrayed him favourably, and the true scale of his wealth and the charges of misuse of public funds are hard to verify. On this view Jiaqing, emperor in name while Heshen dominated the government, made him a scapegoat for the dynasty's problems, and Heshen's supporters were pardoned.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heshen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Salaries grew less and less adequate, and officials came to rely on “squeeze”, irregular payments; in Qianlong’s last years his favourite <b>Heshen</b> became one of the most corrupt officials of the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/societies/qing-china">Social Hierarchy of Qing China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1781" aria-label="Beijing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Heshen"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4765 4154h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4765 4154h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1781">What was here in 1781? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
